  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Increasing knowledge on semiconductors for industry and knowledge institutes</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International corporations are diversifying their production to reduce reliance on China, known as the China+1 strategy. Vietnam is benefiting, especially in high-tech manufacturing like semiconductors, attracting investments from Japan, Korea, America, and now the Netherlands. However, Vietnamese companies lack direct collaboration with foreign firms and skilled labor. Despite advantages like strategic location, low costs, and Foreign Trade Agreements, Vietnam lacks in infrastructure and skilled workforce. To develop its semiconductor industry, Vietnam needs workforce training and international collaboration.The Netherlands has strategic interest in boosting Vietnam&apos;s semiconductor industry to enhance global supply diversity. The Eindhoven University of Technology (TU/e) is organizing a semiconductor talent program open to Vietnamese students, amongst other, aiming to provide insights, networking, and knowledge exchange. The Netherlands embassy in Vietnam is facilitating the participation of five Vietnamese students in the program, with follow-up sessions planned for knowledge sharing and networking at local universities in Vietnam.</narrative>
  </description>
